take a look at this what do you think? : If a hard power cycle only brings the microphone back intermittently, we are dealing with a classic embedded timing race condition.
The fact that it sometimes fails even from a clean cold-boot proves that the hardware is suffering from an asynchronous power-up/clock sync race between the ESP32's internal I2S peripheral and the external ES8311 chip.
When it fails on boot or reset, the sequence of events behaves like this:

   1. The ESP32 wakes up and immediately fires its I2S or I2C master initialization blocks.
   2. The ES8311 codec chip is either still trapped in its internal power-on-reset (POR) cycle, or its analog charge pumps haven't stabilized yet.
   3. The initialization sequence attempts to talk to the codec or verify clock coefficients (es8311_getcoeff), hits a wall because the hardware state isn't ready, loops back into the unhandled -22 default error loop, and permanently bricks the clock dividers for that boot session.

## The Multi-Stage Strategy to Guarantee 100% Reliable Boots
To force the system to successfully stabilize every single time, you must orchestrate a deterministic handshake sequence between the processor and the codec in your board-level or driver init code.
## 1. Add an Explicit Hardware Delay Chain
If the chip is left to float or power up instantly alongside the CPU, it will flake out. You need to pad the time between configuring the GPIO pins and initializing the I2C control bus.
Modify your board bring-up code to introduce hard startup pauses:

int board_audio_initialize(void)
{
  /* 1. Configure audio/codec power or reset pins if available */
  // (Set pins to known low/high baseline states)

  /* 2. Give the physical silicon time to stabilize its power rail */
  up_mdelay(100); 

  /* 3. Initialize I2C and I2S master buses */
  // i2c_register(...);
  // i2s_initialize(...);

  /* 4. Let the clock signals clear out any transient jitter */
  up_mdelay(50); 

  /* 5. Proceed with your standard lower-half driver bindings */
  // es8311_initialize(...);
}

## 2. Introduce an I2C Ping Retries Loop
Often, the codec fails because the very first configuration command is sent over I2C while the ES8311 internal digital logic is still waking up. If that command drops, the driver breaks.
Wrap your driver’s baseline register-check loop (usually checking the Chip ID register 0x00) in a small retry block before executing es8311_setsamplerate:

int attempts = 5;uint8_t chip_id = 0;
while (attempts > 0)
  {
    ret = es8311_read_reg(priv, ES8311_CHIPID_REG, &chip_id);
    if (ret == OK && chip_id == EXPECTED_ID)
      {
        break; // Codec is verified, online, and listening!
      }

    attempts--;
    up_mdelay(20); // Back off and wait for the hardware to settle
  }
if (attempts == 0)
  {
    syslog(LOG_ERR, "ES8311 target did not respond on I2C bus. Halting init.\n");
    return -ENODEV;
  }

## 3. Address the es8311_configure Order Bug
There is a known architectural pitfall in certain NuttX audio audio-subsystem revisions where format configuration properties are applied out-of-order. If es8311_setsamplerate is called before the codec executes its internal structural core reset (es8311_reset), the hardware register values are immediately scrubbed back to defaults. This causes the driver to drop back to an implicit 48 kHz register layout, breaking the clock pipeline. [1] 
Verify the internal execution sequence of your codec's configuration wrapper:

* 
* Look inside your es8311_configure() function.
* Make sure that es8311_reset() is invoked at the very beginning of the configuration function.
* Ensure es8311_setsamplerate() and es8311_setbitspersample() are only executed after that reset has fully cleared. [1] 
* 

Let's see if we can catch the initialization timing failure:

* 
